{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_019czm4EHx2aJxpUuUZeE6ak","type":"tool_result","content":"7:in writing, review, audit, and revision. Distinguish statistical/performance failures from\n32:Implementation-specific claims require inspectable source evidence for the relevant evaluated implementation; cite readable files and locations (`./source_evidence/` when available). Hashes and previous audit verdicts identify provenance, not substantive support for implementation details. Details with unavailable source evidence must be removed or qualified; family-level alignment alone does not establish exact implementation or source identity.\n392:    \"evidence\": \"paper_evidence_audit_feedback.json; source_evidence/downstream/program/src/program.py; paper_step.py STRATEGY_FIGURE_TYPE_ALIASES\",\n396:    \"summary\": \"Repaired audit findings (evaluator-source provenance qualified as stated protocol; unscored cause = not recorded; mechanisms recast as hypotheses) and the figure_type mismatch (strategy types normalized to schema enum grouped_bar/composite). Validators: tex refs OK, claims 24 OK, figures OK, evidence dangling 0; manual build 14 pp, body 9 pp.\",\n484:Summary: Citation coverage is dense overall (all 27 bib entries cited), but three paragraph-specific gaps remain: the upper-bound/auditing positioning of the verified sizes, the corruption-robust ranking comparator family for flip perturbations, and benchmark context for the NBA sports design.\n490:- method-upper-bound-auditing | method | high | positioning\n491:  reason: The paper's main interpretive caveat is that every reported size is an upper bound from a one-step influence ordering, and that the true minimum 'may be smaller, possibly by a lot'. The 'What a verified size means' paragraph supports this with a single citation (low-dimensional OLS auditing). The 'Upper bounds, not minima' limitation paragraph has no citation. Reviewers who know the drop-data literature will